window.docContents[79].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Transition-to-examples-of-representations/#Chapter2___Discussion_examples_intro":{"contents":"Here are some examples of representations.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.3. Representations\tTransition to examples of representations","header":"Transition to examples of representations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Transition-to-examples-of-representations/#Chapter2___Discussion_examples_intro"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Section-3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras-___-heading-and-setup/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___3":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.3. Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\tSection 3.3: Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras — heading and setup","header":"Section 3.3: Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras — heading and setup","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Section-3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras-___-heading-and-setup/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___3"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Representation-of-an-algebra-_LPAR_left-A-module_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Definition2___3___1___heading-1":{"contents":"Definition 2.3.1. A representation of an algebra A (also called a left A-module) is a vector space V together with a homomorphism of algebras \\rho : A \\to \\operatorname{End} V.\n\nSimilarly, a right A-module is a space V equipped with an antihomomorphism \\rho : A \\to \\operatorname{End} V; i.e., \\rho satisfies \\rho(ab) = \\rho(b)\\rho(a) and \\rho(1) = 1.\n\nThe usual abbreviated notation for \\rho(a)v is av for a left module and va for a right module. Then the property that \\rho is an (anti)homomorphism can be written as a kind of associativity law: (ab)v = a(bv) for left modules, and (va)b = v(ab) for right modules.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.3. Representations\tRepresentation of an algebra (left A-module)","header":"2.3. Representations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Representation-of-an-algebra-_LPAR_left-A-module_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Definition2___3___1___heading-1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/Section-4___8___-Character-tables-___-definition-and-examples-of-S_3___-A_4/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___8___heading-1":{"contents":"The characters of all the irreducible representations of a finite group can be arranged into a character table, with conjugacy classes of elements as the columns and characters as the rows. More specifically, the first row in a character table lists representatives of conjugacy classes, the second one lists the numbers of elements in the conjugacy classes, and the other rows list the values of the characters on the conjugacy classes. Due to Theorems 4.5.1 and 4.5.4, the rows and columns of a character table are orthonormal with respect to the appropriate inner products.\n\nNote that in any character table, the row corresponding to the trivial representation consists of ones, and the column corresponding\nto the neutral element consists of the dimensions of the representations.\n\nHere is, for example, the character table of S_3:\n\n* S_3* Id* (12)* (123)\n* #* 1* 3* 2\n* \\mathbb{C}_+* 1* 1* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}_-* 1* -1* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}^2* 2* 0* -1\n\nIt is obtained by explicitly computing traces in the irreducible representations.\n\nFor another example consider A_4, the group of even permutations of four items. There are three 1-dimensional representations (as A_4 has a normal subgroup \\mathbb{Z}_2 \\oplus \\mathbb{Z}_2 and A_4/(\\mathbb{Z}_2 \\oplus \\mathbb{Z}_2) = \\mathbb{Z}_3). Since there are four conjugacy classes in total, there is one more irreducible representation of dimension 3. Finally, the character table is\n\n* A_4* Id* (123)* (132)* (12)(34)\n* #* 1* 4* 4* 3\n* \\mathbb{C}* 1* 1* 1* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}_\\epsilon* 1* \\epsilon* \\epsilon^2* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}_{\\epsilon^2}* 1* \\epsilon^2* \\epsilon* 1\n* \\mathbb{C}^3* 3* 0* 0* -1\n\nwhere \\epsilon = \\exp(\\frac{2\\pi i}{3}).\n\nThe last row can be computed using the orthogonality of rows. Another way to compute the last row is to note that \\mathbb{C}^3 is the representation of A_4 by rotations of the regular tetrahedron: in this case (123), (132) are the rotations by 120^0 and 240^0 around a perpendicular to a face of the tetrahedron, while (12)(34) is the rotation by 180^0 around an axis perpendicular to two opposite edges.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.8. Character tables, examples\tSection 4.8: Character tables — definition and examples of S_3, A_4","header":"4.8. Character tables, examples","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/Section-4___8___-Character-tables-___-definition-and-examples-of-S_3___-A_4/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___8___heading-1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/Section-5___22___-The-characters-of-L_lambda/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___--Section-5___22___-The-characters-of-L_lambda":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.22. The characters of $L_\\lambda$","header":"Section 5.22: The characters of L_lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/Section-5___22___-The-characters-of-L_lambda/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___--Section-5___22___-The-characters-of-L_lambda"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___4___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.4. Burnside's theorem\tSection 5.4: Burnside's theorem","header":"5.4. Burnside's theorem","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___4___heading-1"}});
